```java
// WIDENING — implicit, no cast needed, no data loss
int i = 100;
long l = i;     // int to long — automatic
double d = i;   // int to double — automatic

// NARROWING — explicit cast required, may lose data
double pi = 3.14159;
int truncated = (int) pi;  // 3 — decimal part TRUNCATED (not rounded)
System.out.println(truncated);  // 3

byte b = (byte) 200;  // 200 > byte max (127) — overflow wraps around
System.out.println(b);  // -56 (unexpected!)
```

Widening order: `byte → short → int → long → float → double`

```java
Object obj = "Hello";         // String stored as Object reference
String s = (String) obj;       // OK — actual type IS String
Integer i = (Integer) obj;     // ClassCastException! actual type is String, not Integer

// Safe pattern — check before casting
if (obj instanceof String) {
    String str = (String) obj;  // safe
}

// Java 16+ pattern matching instanceof (cleaner)
if (obj instanceof String str) {
    System.out.println(str.toUpperCase());  // str is already cast
}
```

**Upcasting** (Dog → Animal) is always safe and implicit. **Downcasting** (Animal → Dog) is risky and must be explicit — it only works if the actual runtime object is indeed a Dog.

```java
// String to int
String s = "42";
int num = Integer.parseInt(s);         // returns primitive int
Integer numObj = Integer.valueOf(s);   // returns Integer object

// Handle invalid input
try {
    int n = Integer.parseInt("abc");   // throws NumberFormatException
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    System.out.println("Not a number");
}

// int to String
int n = 42;
String s1 = String.valueOf(n);         // preferred
String s2 = Integer.toString(n);
String s3 = "" + n;                    // works but less clear
```

```java
double d1 = 9.99;
int i1 = (int) d1;      // 9 — decimal truncated, NOT rounded

double d2 = -3.7;
int i2 = (int) d2;      // -3 — truncates toward zero

// To round properly:
int rounded = (int) Math.round(9.99);   // 10
int ceiling = (int) Math.ceil(9.1);      // 10
int floor = (int) Math.floor(9.9);       // 9

// Truncation always goes TOWARD ZERO:
// (int) 3.9 = 3
// (int) -3.9 = -3 (not -4!)
```

```java
byte b1 = 10, b2 = 20;
// byte b3 = b1 + b2;  // COMPILE ERROR! b1+b2 is promoted to int
byte b3 = (byte)(b1 + b2);  // explicit cast needed

// int + long = long
int i = 100;
long l = 200L;
long result = i + l;   // i is promoted to long

// int + double = double
int x = 5;
double d = 2.5;
double r = x + d;  // x promoted to double, result is 7.5

// int / int = int (truncated!)
System.out.println(7 / 2);    // 3, not 3.5
System.out.println(7.0 / 2);  // 3.5 (one operand is double)
```

```java
// This is a COMPILE ERROR — String and int are unrelated
// int n = (int) "42";  // ERROR: cannot cast String to int

// This is the correct approach — parsing
int n = Integer.parseInt("42");   // reads "42" and returns integer 42

// Casting works between numeric types:
long l = 100L;
int i = (int) l;   // valid — both are numeric types in the same hierarchy

// Casting works in class hierarchies:
Animal a = new Dog();
Dog d = (Dog) a;   // valid downcast
```

Parsing = interpreting a String's text content as a number. Casting = converting between compatible types in the same type hierarchy.
